course: dictionary
lesson: 12174
title: One word, many meanings: concession
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence shows you which one is meant. The word concession is one of these. «Both sides made concessions, so the strike ended.» The clues are sides, strike and ended: a concession is something each side gives up to reach a deal.
explain: «The cinema sold concession tickets for children.» The words tickets and children point to a cheaper price. Ask yourself what meaning fits, then put your meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense.
explain: «After the vote, she gave a concession speech and congratulated the winner.» Losing the vote and congratulating the winner tell you that a concession here means admitting you have lost. The context gave you the right meaning.
fact: A concession is something you agree to give up or allow in order to end an argument or reach a deal.
fact: A concession speech is when the loser admits they have lost and that the winner is right.
fact: In the UK and Australia, a concession is a cheaper price for certain people, such as children, students or older people.
fact: In the USA and Canada, a concession stand is a small stall at a cinema, stadium or fair that sells food and drinks.
fact: In business and law, a concession is a right given by a government or owner to do business or use land, such as to drill for oil.
exercise: «Both sides made concessions, and the workers went back to work.» What does concession mean here? | A cheap ticket for children | A speech by the winner | *Something each side gave up to reach a deal | A small shop selling popcorn
exercise: «Mum bought two concession tickets at the zoo.» In the UK, why was the price lower? | The tickets were free for everyone | The zoo was closing that day | A farm sold the tickets cheaply | *Children and older people pay a cheaper price
exercise: «The concession stand sold popcorn and fizzy drinks.» Where would you find a concession stand? | In a train station office | *At a cinema, stadium or fair | At a hospital | In a forest
test: «After the election, she gave a concession speech.» What is a concession speech? | A speech about a new park | A speech that makes everyone laugh | *A speech admitting you have lost | A speech about the weather
test: In a peace deal, what does a side do when it makes a concession? | *It gives something up | It buys the land | It leaves the room | It wins the game
exam: «The government gave the company a concession to drill for oil.» What does concession mean here? | *A right to use the land for business | A cheaper price for drilling | A stall selling drinks at the site | An admission of defeat
